// Daily studies 1–12. Schema: t title, k kicker, ref[], tag, c comfortable reading, f fracture, d[] dig, v[[who,text]], b[claim,pushback], q[], s[prompt,left,right], p practice, src[]
export default [
{
t: `The Command`, k: `Genesis 22 · The Binding of Isaac`, ref: [`Genesis 22:1-14`],
tag: `A story most Christians know as “Abraham’s great faith.” Read it as a parent.`,
c: [`God tests Abraham’s faith by asking for Isaac, stops him at the last second, and provides a ram. The lesson: trust God completely; he will provide.`],
f: [`Read it slowly. God *commands* the killing of a child. The narrator tells us it is a test, but Abraham does not know that. He argues for Sodom (Gen 18) and for nothing here — not a word. The next morning he gets up early. Isaac, bound on the altar, never speaks again in the story — and the text never says he came down the mountain with his father (22:19). Whatever you believe the passage is for, it has a body at the centre of it.`, `And then verse 12: “Now I know that you fear God.” If God already knew, the test is theatre. If God did not know, your doctrine of divine foreknowledge is in trouble.`],
d: [`**The world behind the text.** Child sacrifice appears in Israel’s own sources more often than comfortable preaching admits. Exodus 22:29 says “the firstborn of your sons you shall give to me.” Micah 6:7 asks whether God might want “my firstborn for my transgression.” Jeremiah 7:31 and 19:5 insist that God “did not command” the burning of children in Topheth, “nor did it come into my mind” — a denial that only makes sense if somebody was claiming he did. Ezekiel 20:25–26 goes further and has God say that he gave “statutes that were not good” in order to horrify his people. Jon Levenson’s *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son* argues that the idea of the firstborn belonging to God, and of God’s beloved son passing through death, runs deep in Israelite tradition — and that Genesis 22 is where the tradition changes shape, not where it begins.`, `**Two traditional readings.** The story is (a) the *abolition* of child sacrifice — God provides the substitute — or (b) the *ultimate demand* of faith. Rabbinic readers lingered over Abraham’s silence. Christians, from the New Testament on, read the story as a type of the Father giving the Son (Rom 8:32; John 3:16), which makes God the one who does not stop the knife. Kierkegaard’s *Fear and Trembling* argues the reverse of the Sunday-school moral: if Abraham is a hero, he is a murderer by every ethical measure, and the only way to defend him is to claim a private, unjustifiable relation to God that suspends ethics.`, `**Is the story Genesis’s own critique?** Some scholars notice that the Hebrew has God *test* (nissah) and that Abraham’s final name for the place is “The LORD will provide” — a theological claim *against* sacrifice. Others note the angel, not God, calls off the sacrifice, which is a way of preserving both the command and its reversal.`],
v: [[`Immanuel Kant`, `In *The Conflict of the Faculties* (1798), Kant says Abraham should have answered the voice: that I must not kill my son is certain; that you, the voice, are God, is not. If that is right, a command to do wrong is itself evidence the voice is not God.`], [`Søren Kierkegaard`, `In *Fear and Trembling* (1843) he says he cannot understand Abraham; he can only marvel at him. He refuses to soften it: either faith can demand what ethics forbids, or Abraham is lost.`], [`The Qur’an (37:99–111)`, `Retells the story as a dream-command. The son is not named in the text, and the son — not just the father — consents (“do what you are commanded”). The weight shifts onto shared submission, not a father’s solitary obedience.`], [`Jon D. Levenson (Jewish scholar)`, `Treats the story as the hinge of Israelite memory: a son consecrated and returned. The New Testament’s Christology, he argues, reads backward into this older pattern rather than inventing a new one.`]],
b: [`The book reaches the same story through Kierkegaard: the “knight of faith” turns from the universal to the Absolute, a “teleological suspension of the ethical,” in “fear and trembling.” For Mantz, Abraham is the model of a relation with the Absolute that cannot be rationally justified or explained to anyone else, yet is not irrational.`, `This is where the book is most vulnerable to its own rule. In “A Note to the Critic” it says “because the book says so” is never valid and authority is not an argument. But the knight of faith is exactly one who acts on a command no one else can check. How does the book distinguish Abraham from someone who hears the same voice and kills? Kant’s question is not answered by Kierkegaard’s awe. And if the Absolute has exhaustive foreknowledge, what does “now I know” mean?`],
q: [`If you heard a voice commanding what you knew to be evil, what would you need to hear to believe it was God? What would that tell you about where your morality actually lives?`, `Is Genesis 22 God’s rejection of child sacrifice, or its sublimation into a spiritual demand? What evidence from the passage supports your answer?`, `Isaac is silent. What does it do to the story to retell it from his point of view?`, `Does the doctrine that God knows everything make the “test” coherent, or empty?`],
s: [`Abraham was right to obey.`, `Abraham was wrong; he should have refused.`, `Abraham was right; the command was legitimate`],
p: `Today, sit for five minutes with the thought you least want to think about this passage. Write it in one sentence. Do not resolve it.`,
src: [`Jon D. Levenson, *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son* (Yale, 1993)`, `Søren Kierkegaard, *Fear and Trembling* (1843)`, `Immanuel Kant, *The Conflict of the Faculties* (1798)`, `Walter Brueggemann, *Genesis* (Interpretation commentary, 1982)`]
},
{
t: `Two Creations, One Editor`, k: `Genesis 1–2 · Origins`, ref: [`Genesis 1:1-5`, `Genesis 2:4-9`],
tag: `Why does the Bible open with two stories that don’t agree on the order of things?`,
c: [`Genesis 1 gives the big picture, Genesis 2 zooms in on Adam and Eve. They complement each other.`],
f: [`In Genesis 1, plants appear on day three, animals on days five and six, and humans — male and female together — last. In Genesis 2, the man is made *first*, before plants have sprouted (2:5), then the animals are made *to find him a companion*, and the woman is made last. In chapter 1, God speaks things into being with a word. In chapter 2, God forms a man from dust like a potter and breathes into his nostrils, walks in a garden, and is surprised that the animals aren’t suitable companions. They are not two zoom levels of one story. They are two stories.`],
d: [`**Why they sit together.** Most critical scholars assign Genesis 1:1–2:3 to the Priestly tradition (a liturgical, ordered, cosmic account, perhaps exilic) and 2:4 onward to the older Yahwist tradition (earthy, anthropomorphic). A later editor placed them side by side, evidently unbothered by the discrepancy. That editor’s calm is itself a datum: whatever the compilers thought “truth” meant for a creation story, it was not harmony of sequence.`, `**Echoes of older worlds.** The Babylonian *Enuma Elish* tells of Marduk splitting the sea-goddess Tiamat to make the heavens and earth; the Hebrew *tehom* (“the deep,” Gen 1:2) is a deliberately demythologised cousin of Tiamat. The Atrahasis epic has humans made from clay and divine blood to do the gods’ labour; Genesis 2:7 preserves the clay but swaps the purpose. John Walton (an evangelical) argues Genesis 1 is a *temple-inauguration* text — the cosmos as God’s sanctuary — not a material-origins account. Whether you like that or not, it is a serious attempt to read the text on its own terms.`, `**What’s at stake.** If you read Genesis 1 as a chronology, you must explain light before the sun (day one vs day four) and the dome (*raqia*) holding back waters above. If you read it as polemical theology — one God, no rivals, humanity as image-bearers, Sabbath at the climax — the text’s sophisticated claims come into focus. But then you must also ask what *else* in Scripture is operating that way.`],
v: [[`Enuma Elish (Babylon, c. 12th c. BCE or earlier)`, `A creation epic in which the god Marduk defeats chaos (Tiamat) and builds the world from her body; humanity is made from a rebel god’s blood to serve the gods. Genesis 1 never mentions a battle. Its silence reads like a deliberate rebuke.`], [`John H. Walton`, `Argues that in the ancient Near East, to “create” meant to assign function and order, not to manufacture matter. Genesis 1 describes God giving the cosmos its roles and then taking up residence — the Sabbath is “rest” in a temple.`], [`2 Maccabees 7:28`, `The first text in the Jewish scriptures that clearly says God made everything “out of things that did not exist.” Creation *ex nihilo* is a developed doctrine, not a plain reading of Genesis 1.`]],
b: [`Mantz writes that matter has not always existed, “contrary to the Platonic view,” and that the Absolute created matter *ex nihilo*. He also says the Scriptures “were never meant to substitute or even affirm scientific and historical tenets” but to show the nature of the Absolute’s relation with *Mensch* — so Genesis can be read for what it says about relation rather than as geology.`, `The book is consistent here: infallibility rather than inerrancy. But the claim “ex nihilo” is more than Genesis 1 says. It relies on 2 Maccabees and later philosophy. If you grant that doctrine its development after the text, are you also willing to grant that doctrines like the Trinity developed after the text? And if infallibility covers “all matters upon which it touches,” who decides which matters it touches?`],
q: [`If the editor did not mind the two orders, what does that tell you about what the editor thought a creation story was for?`, `What is lost if Genesis 1 is a polemic against Babylonian myth? What is gained?`, `Where else in the Bible do you instinctively allow a non-literal reading? Why there and not here?`],
s: [`How literal should Genesis 1–2 be read?`, `Not literal at all`, `Fully literal`],
p: `Read Genesis 1 aloud slowly, as liturgy, with no attempt to analyse. Notice what the repetition does to you.`,
src: [`John H. Walton, *The Lost World of Genesis One* (IVP, 2009)`, `Mark S. Smith, *The Priestly Vision of Genesis 1* (Fortress, 2010)`, `Richard Elliott Friedman, *Who Wrote the Bible?* (1987)`, `Jon D. Levenson, *Creation and the Persistence of Evil* (1988)`]
},
{
t: `The Ban`, k: `Deuteronomy 7 · Joshua 6 · Divine violence`, ref: [`Deuteronomy 7:1-6`, `Joshua 6:15-21`],
tag: `The passage that makes more people leave the faith than any other.`,
c: [`The Canaanites were exceedingly wicked; God used Israel as an instrument of judgment, just as he later used Babylon to judge Israel.`],
f: [`Joshua 6:21: “they devoted all in the city to destruction, both men and women, young and old, oxen, sheep, and donkeys.” Deuteronomy 7:2 forbids mercy: “show them no mercy.” Whatever else is true, a book that claims to reveal the character of God contains instructions for the destruction of whole populations, with infants. If you would call it genocide when anyone else did it, you must say why this is different — or that the text doesn’t mean what it appears to mean.`],
d: [`**What the archaeology says.** Excavations at Jericho (Kenyon, later reassessments) do not show a walled city destroyed by Israel in the late Bronze Age; Ai appears to have been uninhabited. Most archaeologists see Canaanite and Israelite culture as largely continuous: Israelites emerged from within Canaan, not from outside. If so, the conquest as written never happened as written.`, `**Hyperbole?** Conservative scholars (Copan, Flannagan; also Younger on ancient conquest accounts) point out that Near Eastern war-texts routinely claim total destruction using stock phrases. Joshua 10:40 says Joshua “left no one remaining” — yet Judges 1 has the same peoples still in the land. The text itself is *saying and unsaying* total annihilation. If it’s rhetoric, it was rhetoric the editors cherished.`, `**Critics respond:** That the language is exaggerated does not make the *theology* less violent. It still pictures God commanding total war. Eric Seibert (*Disturbing Divine Behavior*) argues Christians must read the portrayals critically, through the lens of Jesus, as human understandings of God that need correcting. Others (e.g., Hector Avalos in *Fighting Words*, Thom Stark in *The Human Faces of God*) accuse conservative defenders of an apologetic that would never be tolerated if applied to another culture’s scriptures.`, `**The Moabite mirror.** The Mesha Stele (9th c. BCE, Moab) boasts that Chemosh commanded the king to “take Nebo from Israel” and that he devoted its people (using the same Hebrew word, *herem*). The logic of divine war was the common currency of the region. Is the biblical version different because it is true, or because it is ours?`],
v: [[`Origen of Alexandria (c. 230 CE)`, `Wrestled with the same problem: if Joshua’s wars are read literally, they are monstrous. Origen insisted they be read allegorically, as Christ’s war against sin within us.`], [`Marcion (c. 140 CE)`, `Took the opposite route: the God of the Old Testament is a lesser, harsh deity, utterly different from the Father of Jesus. The church condemned him — but his question never left.`], [`Eric Seibert`, `Argues that when a biblical portrayal of God conflicts with Jesus’ revelation, Christians must side with Jesus and treat the earlier portrayal as a human perspective, not a transcript.`], [`Paul Copan & Matthew Flannagan`, `Maintain that the Bible’s war-rhetoric is hyperbolic, that Canaanite society was morally corrupt, and that the text is describing judgment, not ethnic cleansing. Their critics find the reply unconvincing.`]],
b: [`The book does not address the conquest directly, but its argument about evil does. In “The Wickedness on Earth,” Mantz writes that the Absolute “formed light and created darkness” and that Lucifer does not exist: “it is the Absolute who … kills and brings to life.” The author goes further and says there is no culprit — “good and bad are merely subjective perspectives.” If so, the Canaanites’ destruction is not an exception to God’s character; it is part of how the Absolute governs.`, `That is the hardest corner of the book to defend. It says no moral standard exists without the Absolute (Part III, 1.II) — but the commands at issue are from the Absolute. If the Absolute’s commands *define* morality, then “good” has no content other than “whatever is commanded,” and the Euthyphro problem returns. If they don’t, we have a standard to judge them by, and we are back to Seibert’s dilemma. Which horn is yours?`],
q: [`If a modern government justified a massacre by citing divine command, what would you say? Does the answer change with time?`, `What would be lost from your faith if the conquest never happened historically? What would be gained?`, `Joshua says no one was left; Judges says they were still there. What does an editor do who keeps both?`, `If Jesus is the clearest revelation of God, does the conquest have to be read through him? What does that do to the doctrine of Scripture?`],
s: [`The conquest narratives faithfully report what God commanded.`, `They are human perspective, not God’s command`, `They are an accurate record of a divine command`],
p: `Read Luke 6:27–36 immediately after Joshua 6. Notice what happens in your body, and write it down.`,
src: [`Paul Copan & Matthew Flannagan, *Did God Really Command Genocide?* (Baker, 2014)`, `Eric A. Seibert, *Disturbing Divine Behavior* (Fortress, 2009)`, `K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Ancient Conquest Accounts* (JSOT, 1990)`, `Israel Finkelstein & Neil Silberman, *The Bible Unearthed* (Free Press, 2001)`]
},
{
t: `The Wager and the Whirlwind`, k: `Job 1–2 · 38 · 42 · Suffering and God`, ref: [`Job 1:6-12`, `Job 38:1-11`, `Job 42:7-9`],
tag: `God hands a righteous man over to suffering to win a bet. Then answers a question he was never asked.`,
c: [`Job suffers, stays faithful, and is restored. The lesson: trust God’s ways even when you can’t understand.`],
f: [`The prologue is not subtle: God points to Job as proof of human faithfulness, the *satan* says it’s only because he’s rewarded, and God allows him to destroy Job’s children, servants, and health to see what happens. Job never learns of the wager. When God finally speaks, he doesn’t explain, justify or apologise. He asks forty questions about the cosmos — and never mentions Job’s suffering. Then, astonishingly, in 42:7 God says Job has spoken *rightly* about him, and the friends have not. The friends defended God with theology. God sides with the man who accused him.`],
d: [`**A text in two layers.** The folktale frame (chs 1–2; 42:7–17) is older; the poetry between is later and radically different. In the frame, Job is “patient.” In the poetry, he is furious and demanding a lawsuit against God. The Epilogue’s restoration — double sons, double sheep — is an awkward fit with the cry of the poems. Many scholars see the compiler folding a challenge to the tradition into a story that, at least at first, reinforces it.`, `**The satan.** In Job the figure is “ha-satan,” “the accuser” — a member of the divine council, a kind of prosecutor, not the devil. He appears only with God’s permission and acts within limits God sets. The devil of popular imagination, the cosmic rebel, is a later development. Reading Job as the devil’s plot misses that it is *God’s* decision to hand Job over (2:3 says God was “incited”).`, `**The speeches.** God’s answer in 38–41 is not a theodicy but a revelation of scale: Behemoth, Leviathan, the wild ox, the ostrich who is deprived of wisdom. It is a world overflowing with wild, indifferent, beautiful things God loves and humans cannot govern. Some read this as comfort (God is bigger than your grief), others as terror (the universe has no centre in you). Both readings are available in the text.`],
v: [[`Gustavo Gutiérrez (liberation theologian)`, `In *On Job* he argues the book’s lesson is that one can speak to God out of unjust suffering without ceasing to believe — and that the poor, not the comfortable, are Job’s true interpreters.`], [`Carl Jung, *Answer to Job* (1952)`, `Reads God as unconscious of his own darkness in the book, learning something through Job’s protest. Heretical, brilliant, and still discussed.`], [`The Talmud (Bava Batra 15a–16b)`, `Debates whether Job ever existed, whether he was a Gentile, whether he was righteous, and whether he blasphemed — a rabbinic tradition that took the book’s dangerous questions seriously.`], [`Elie Wiesel`, `In *The Trial of God* (1979), written after the Holocaust, imagines a Purim play in which survivors put God on trial, and the only defender is a figure who may be the devil.`]],
b: [`Mantz’s answer to the problem of evil is a free-will theodicy: evil is *privatio boni*; the Absolute made *Mensch* free for genuine relation; a world without pain would have no free will; “There must exist either free will and a fallen Earth, or no will but a perfect world.” He also denies that Satan exists, so Job’s *satan* would be a title for an adversarial role under the Absolute’s control. And he insists that “the Absolute does not hover within the world … manipulating events.”`, `But Job’s story breaks both halves of that. The prologue depicts God *actively handing Job over* — not a world left alone, but one in which God authorises specific suffering to test a specific man. How does this fit a view of divine non-interference? And the free-will defence explains moral evil (humans harming humans) — but Job’s children die in a windstorm and his body in disease. What does free will explain about *that*? The book says natural evil “does not exist” because it is merely natural; a grieving parent will not find that persuasive.`],
q: [`What is the difference between a theodicy that defends God and a response that stays with the sufferer? Which one do the friends offer?`, `Why do you think the author let God say the friends spoke wrongly, and Job rightly? What kind of religion does that permit?`, `God never answers Job’s question. Did Job get an answer anyway?`, `If a God arranged a wager like this today, what would you call him?`],
s: [`Job’s suffering was justified.`, `Not justified`, `Justified`],
p: `Write a letter you would send to God if you could be as honest as Job. Don’t send it. Don’t tear it up.`,
src: [`Gustavo Gutiérrez, *On Job: God-Talk and the Suffering of the Innocent* (1987)`, `Carol Newsom, *The Book of Job: A Contest of Moral Imaginations* (Oxford, 2003)`, `Elie Wiesel, *The Trial of God* (1979)`, `Marilyn McCord Adams, *Horrendous Evils and the Goodness of God* (1999)`]
},
{
t: `Vapour`, k: `Ecclesiastes 1–3 · Meaning`, ref: [`Ecclesiastes 1:1-11`, `Ecclesiastes 3:16-22`],
tag: `A book of the Bible that thinks life is meaningless — and was kept anyway.`,
c: [`Life without God is meaningless; the Preacher shows the futility of living apart from him. The last verses supply the moral.`],
f: [`“Vanity of vanities” translates *hevel*: breath, vapour, a puff of air. It occurs about thirty-eight times. Qohelet’s argument is brutal: the wise and the fool die alike; humans and beasts share one fate; no one can know whether the human spirit rises or the animal’s sinks (3:21); the dead know nothing (9:5). The book that is supposed to be inspired contains an author who doubts the afterlife, and nearly every sermon on it has to rescue it from itself. Many scholars think the closing verses (12:13–14) were added later by someone who needed to calm the reader down.`],
d: [`**Which tradition does he belong to?** The Hebrew Bible’s dominant view of death is Sheol — a shadow-land of silence, not reward or punishment. Resurrection arrives late (Daniel 12, second century BCE). Qohelet is pre-resurrection in his view; he is less a sceptic than a faithful Israelite stating plainly what the older tradition believed. The distance between him and later Christian heaven is a measure of how much development occurred.`, `**His method.** He tests: pleasure (ch 2), work, wisdom, wealth, justice. “I said in my heart” appears repeatedly. His standard is observation, not revelation. This is an author who treats experience as evidence. The tension between that and prophetic “thus says the LORD” is built into the canon.`, `**Joy as the answer?** Several times (2:24; 3:12–13; 5:18; 9:7–9) Qohelet recommends eating, drinking, working, loving your spouse, as *the portion God gives* — an ethic of finite, present gratitude without metaphysical guarantees. Some read this as shallow; others see a deep wisdom: meaning not as a conclusion but as a gift in the present.`],
v: [[`Buddhism (anicca)`, `The Buddha’s teaching that all conditioned things are impermanent and that clinging to permanence causes suffering has a parallel in Qohelet’s *hevel*. The prescriptions differ, but the diagnosis is startlingly close.`], [`Albert Camus, *The Myth of Sisyphus* (1942)`, `Asks whether life is worth living in a universe without meaning, and answers with defiant, lucid revolt. Qohelet reaches a quieter conclusion: enjoy the bread and the wine.`], [`Gilgamesh, Tablet X (Old Babylonian)`, `The alewife tells Gilgamesh to stop chasing immortality: fill your belly, make merry, cherish the child who holds your hand. The advice is strikingly close to what Qohelet says in 9:7–9.`]],
b: [`For Mantz, the Absolute itself faces a version of this crisis: “He who increases knowledge increases suffering: thus the Absolute … is infinitely despairing” — a line that is almost Ecclesiastes 1:18. The book proposes that the Absolute has no natural purpose, and must fashion “a tailored and personal meaning” — relation — to give itself a reason to be. Meaning is therefore constructed, not discovered: “natural or true purpose is that of which the universe speaks … For the universe does not speak.”`, `This is a bold move — and the pushback is sharp. If meaning is fabricated even for the Absolute, why would *Mensch’s* relation to such a being deliver more than Qohelet’s bread-and-wine? And a God who needs us in order not to be in despair is radically different from the God of Psalm 50:12 (“If I were hungry, I would not tell you”). Is the book’s Absolute a stronger answer to Ecclesiastes — or a projection of it onto heaven?`],
q: [`Which is more honest: Qohelet’s doubt about the afterlife, or the confident hope of the New Testament? Can you hold both?`, `What would it mean to live the “bread and wine” ethic as a spiritual practice?`, `Why do you think the compilers of the canon kept a book like this?`],
s: [`Ecclesiastes is a faithful book.`, `Faithless: it undermines faith`, `Deeply faithful: it models honest faith`],
p: `Eat one meal today slowly, aware that you are not entitled to it. Say thank you to whoever you thank.`,
src: [`Michael V. Fox, *A Time to Tear Down and a Time to Build Up* (Eerdmans, 1999)`, `Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible, 1997)`, `Albert Camus, *The Myth of Sisyphus* (1942)`, `Jon D. Levenson, *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel* (Yale, 2006)`]
},
{
t: `The Psalm With No Dawn`, k: `Psalm 88 · Lament without resolution`, ref: [`Psalm 88`],
tag: `Almost every lament in the Psalms turns toward hope by the end. This one doesn’t.`,
c: [`Even the psalms of despair end in hope, because God is faithful.`],
f: [`That isn’t true of Psalm 88. It begins with God as “the God of my salvation” — the only note of hope — then moves through drowning, rejection, isolation and ends with the line: “you have caused my beloved and my friend to shun me; my companions have become darkness.” Hebrew has no resolution there. The psalm does not pivot. It stops in the dark. And someone — a scribe, a temple singer, a compiler — kept it in the hymnbook.`],
d: [`**Form.** Laments typically move from complaint to petition to confidence (cf. Psalm 13). Psalm 88 refuses the third stage. Walter Brueggemann calls it the poem of “disorientation” with no “reorientation.” It is the only psalm whose speaker never gives thanks or promises praise (v. 10–12 argue *against* the possibility of praise from the dead).`, `**Theology.** Sheol is not a place where God is worshipped. “Do you work wonders for the dead?” (v. 10). The speaker is not rebelling against God; he is reminding God that his death would cost God a worshipper. This is shocking and old-fashioned and very human.`, `**Use.** Pastorally, the psalm has been a lifeline to people for whom the standard consolations feel like a lie. It teaches that the canonical vocabulary of faith includes a prayer that never recovers within the poem. Some interpreters stress that the first line (“LORD, God of my salvation”) is the only open window; others insist that’s the point of the lament — a sufferer who keeps praying without receiving an answer.`],
v: [[`Walter Brueggemann`, `In *The Message of the Psalms* he treats Psalm 88 as the poem that holds the line where the comfortable cannot: God’s people may be left in darkness and still address God. Faith can be a refusal to stop speaking.`], [`Jewish practice`, `The Talmud (Moed Katan 21a) says a mourner may study Job, Lamentations and the harsh passages of Jeremiah. Grief is given its own canon, and it is not treated as an obstacle to faith.`], [`Teresa of Calcutta’s letters`, `Posthumously published in *Come Be My Light* (2007), reveal decades of felt absence of God. The saint of charity prayed in something close to Psalm 88.`]],
b: [`Mantz’s account of Hades turns on this idea: it “is the place of ‘weeping and gnashing of teeth’ precisely because of the *absence* of the Absolute” — not a spatial absence, but “the relational depletion of the Absolute and *Mensch*.” That is a description very close to Psalm 88’s felt condition.`, `And that is where the pushback bites: by the book’s own definition, a person praying Psalm 88 *is* in Hades — in the experienced absence of the Absolute — and yet they are praying. The psalmist is simultaneously in the place of relational depletion and in relation, calling out. Does the book’s sharp binary between *Glӓubige* and *Unglӓubige*, presence and absence, allow for a faithful believer in the dark? Or is Psalm 88 evidence that faith contains absence, without being damned by it?`],
q: [`Have you ever prayed something that didn’t resolve? What did you do with the silence?`, `Why might a religious community choose to preserve a text with no hope?`, `If you were editing the Bible, would you have left this in?`],
s: [`Psalm 88 is a model of faith.`, `Not a model — it shows faith failing`, `A model: faith speaking in the dark`],
p: `Read Psalm 88 aloud, in full, slowly. Do not add a closing line. Sit in the silence for a minute.`,
src: [`Walter Brueggemann, *The Message of the Psalms* (Augsburg, 1984)`, `Mother Teresa, *Come Be My Light*, ed. Brian Kolodiejchuk (2007)`, `Robert Alter, *The Book of Psalms: A Translation with Commentary* (2007)`]
},
{
t: `Who Incited David?`, k: `2 Samuel 24 · 1 Chronicles 21 · The origin of Satan`, ref: [`2 Samuel 24:1-4`, `1 Chronicles 21:1-4`],
tag: `Two accounts of the same event, one verse apart in meaning.`,
c: [`The two accounts describe the same event from complementary angles: God allowed Satan to tempt David.`],
f: [`2 Samuel 24:1: “The anger of the LORD was kindled against Israel, and he incited David against them, saying, ‘Go, number Israel and Judah.’” 1 Chronicles 21:1: “Satan stood up against Israel and incited David to number Israel.” The very *same word* for “incited.” Then God punishes David with a plague that kills 70,000 — for doing what God (or Satan) induced him to do. The Chronicler, writing centuries later, has quietly *replaced* God with Satan in the sentence.`],
d: [`**Development of the figure.** In Numbers 22:22 the angel of the LORD is called a *satan* — an adversary — to Balaam. In Job 1–2 and Zechariah 3, “ha-satan” is a courtroom title. Here in 1 Chronicles 21:1, “Satan” appears without the article: arguably the first time the word functions as a proper name. Between the Samuel and Chronicles accounts, Judaism’s thinking about the divine origin of evil changed. The Chronicler’s theology cannot accept that God would incite a sin and punish it, so he introduces an intermediary.`, `**The pattern of editing.** This is not a one-off. The Chronicler also changes the number of people (2 Sam 24:9 vs 1 Chr 21:5) and the price paid for the threshing floor (50 shekels of silver vs 600 shekels of gold). The Bible edits itself.`, `**Where the devil comes from.** The Hebrew Bible has no cosmic dualism. The idea of a rebel angel — Mastema in Jubilees, Belial at Qumran, Azazel/Shemihazah in the Enoch literature — expands in the Second Temple period, after the exile (when many scholars see Persian Zoroastrian dualism as an influence, though how much is debated). The New Testament inherits a developed Satan. Elaine Pagels (*The Origin of Satan*) argues the figure was also shaped by intra-Jewish conflict and later Christian-Jewish polemic.`],
v: [[`Elaine Pagels`, `Argues that the idea of Satan as the enemy within — not outside — Israel grew from sectarian conflict, and that the Gospels use him to characterise Jesus’ opponents, setting patterns that later turned against Jews and heretics.`], [`Henry Ansgar Kelly, *Satan: A Biography* (2006)`, `Insists that Satan’s “fall,” Lucifer’s identity, and the rebel-angel myth are mostly post-biblical accretions; the Bible’s own picture is of a prosecutor, a tester, and a tempter, not of an archrival to God.`], [`1 Enoch 6–11 (3rd c. BCE onward)`, `Tells of angels (the Watchers) who descend and teach forbidden arts; the source of evil is angelic rebellion, not human sin alone. This became the dominant Second Temple explanation, and it shaped the New Testament’s demonology.`], [`Zoroastrian Yasna 30`, `The Gathas speak of two primal spirits, one choosing good, one evil — a cosmic dualism that Isaiah 45:7 may be rejecting by name (“I form light and create darkness”).`]],
b: [`This is the book’s signature thesis. Mantz writes that “the Devil’s very existence must be challenged against the pillars of reason, rooted in historical analysis.” He argues it is the Absolute that “form[s] light and create[s] darkness” (Isa 45:7), and concludes: “Al-Shayṭān does not exist … *Mensch* alone is Lucifer. *Mensch* alone is al-Shayṭān, the Satan that tempts the heart of *dem Menschen*.” 2 Samuel 24 supports his case: the Absolute incites.`, `The pushback: Mantz reads the older text as the truer one, but he also cites Isaiah 14 and Ezekiel 28 as if they were accounts of Lucifer’s fall before denying Lucifer exists. And he affirms “the Christ” as coming “to unravel and undo the works of the Morning Star” — language that treats Lucifer as real. The New Testament’s Jesus names Satan constantly (Luke 10:18, John 8:44). If the church was wrong about Satan for 2,000 years, why trust it about the Trinity? The *same* historical-critical method that demotes Satan also demotes the Trinity’s Biblical footing.`],
q: [`Which do you find more disturbing — that God incited David, or that someone else did? Why?`, `What does it mean for your faith if the Bible’s own authors changed their minds about where evil comes from?`, `If Satan is not a rebel angel but an adversarial function, does it change how you read Jesus’ temptation?`, `If you accept the Chronicler’s edit, are you free to make your own edits?`],
s: [`Satan is a real, personal being.`, `No: a later development`, `Yes: a real, personal being`],
p: `Make a list of the evils you attribute to “the enemy” or “spiritual attack.” Next to each, write what a world without a devil would have you do about it.`,
src: [`Elaine Pagels, *The Origin of Satan* (Random House, 1995)`, `Henry Ansgar Kelly, *Satan: A Biography* (Cambridge, 2006)`, `Peggy L. Day, *An Adversary in Heaven: śāṭān in the Hebrew Bible* (Harvard Semitic Monographs, 1988)`, `Michael S. Heiser, *The Unseen Realm* (Lexham, 2015)`]
},
{
t: `Hardened`, k: `Exodus 4:21 · Romans 9 · Free will and sovereignty`, ref: [`Exodus 7:1-5`, `Romans 9:14-21`],
tag: `Pharaoh refuses to let Israel go — because God made him refuse.`,
c: [`Pharaoh hardened his own heart first, and then God confirmed his choice.`],
f: [`That’s the standard defence, and it’s partly in the text: in Exodus 7:13, 8:15, 8:32, “Pharaoh hardened his heart.” But Exodus 4:21 and 7:3 announce, *before* the plagues begin, “I will harden Pharaoh’s heart.” And Romans 9:17–18 strips the ambiguity: “So then he has mercy on whomever he wills, and he hardens whomever he wills.” Paul anticipates the objection (“Why then does he still find fault?”) and doesn’t answer it. He says: “Who are you, O man, to answer back to God?”`],
d: [`**Reformed vs Arminian readings.** For Calvinists (Augustine, Calvin, Piper), Romans 9 teaches unconditional election: God chooses individuals for salvation and passes over others. For Arminians (Wesley, Witherington), the chapter is about God’s sovereign choice of *roles* in history (Israel, Pharaoh) and not about individual eternal destinies. For others, Paul’s argument is corporate and covenantal; Romans 9–11 is about the fate of Israel and the vindication of God’s word.`, `**The text.** Malachi 1:2–3 (“Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated”) is about nations, in context. Paul quotes it in Romans 9:13 and moves immediately to potter and clay (from Jeremiah 18, where the potter’s freedom is conditional on the clay’s response, but also Isaiah 29:16 and 45:9). Notice Paul’s next move: “vessels of wrath prepared for destruction” — *prepared by whom?* The Greek is a passive participle that can be middle (“prepared themselves”) or passive.`, `**The philosophical stakes.** If God determines all things, how can humans be blamed? The book’s own claim — that free will is “mutually compatible” with meticulous providence — is the compatibilist position; its critics (Open Theists, libertarian free-will defenders) say it empties “freedom.”`],
v: [[`Augustine, *On the Predestination of the Saints* (428 CE)`, `Reads Romans 9 as the clearest statement that grace is not given because of anything foreseen in human beings; faith itself is a gift.`], [`Jacobus Arminius (1560–1609)`, `Argued that God’s election is conditioned on foreknown faith and that grace can be resisted — a protest against Calvinist determinism that reshaped Protestantism.`], [`Origen, *On First Principles* (c. 225)`, `Interprets the hardening of Pharaoh as an educational process: God’s patience hardens the stubborn by the very kindness that softens the willing — as sun hardens clay and melts wax.`], [`Jewish tradition (Exodus Rabbah)`, `Debates whether Pharaoh lost the power to repent after the fifth plague; some sages hold that God does not remove freedom, but that the sinner’s repeated choices eventually close the door.`]],
b: [`On this question Mantz stakes out a firm position: “The Absolute … foreordained and preordained all things, having decreed *toutes les choses* in advance, and knows both intuitively and exhaustively.” And simultaneously: “Mutually compatible … is also free will, which may permeate through perfect predestination without proving logically inconsistent.” The book also says election is “conditional” — “the election married with prostrating faith sanctions redemption” — and “contingent upon belief.”`, `The tension is built in: if the Absolute has decreed every event, including whether a given person prostrates, then conditional election is conditional on a condition the Absolute has also decreed. Compatibilists like Augustine and Calvin accept that and embrace unconditional election. The book appears to want both: exhaustive determination *and* a real, free, blameworthy choice. Does the word “contingent” have meaning if the contingency was fixed before time? Pharaoh’s case is the test.`],
q: [`Do you think Pharaoh was *able* to let Israel go? If not, was he guilty?`, `Which would trouble you more: a God who decrees everything or a God who risks and doesn’t control?`, `Why does Paul refuse to give a philosophical answer — and is that a rhetorical dodge or an honest limit?`],
s: [`God’s sovereignty and human freedom are compatible.`, `Not compatible`, `Fully compatible`],
p: `Make a decision today that you have been avoiding. Notice what you tell yourself about whether it was “you” who chose.`,
src: [`John Piper, *The Justification of God* (Baker, 1983)`, `Ben Witherington III & Darlene Hyatt, *Paul’s Letter to the Romans* (Eerdmans, 2004)`, `Alvin Plantinga, *God, Freedom, and Evil* (1974)`, `William Lane Craig, *The Only Wise God* (1987)`]
},
{
t: `Light and Calamity`, k: `Isaiah 45:1-7 · The one source of everything`, ref: [`Isaiah 45:1-7`],
tag: `Monotheism, at full strength, has no one else to blame.`,
c: [`God is sovereign over everything, even if he doesn’t cause evil directly.`],
f: [`“I form light and create darkness; I make well-being and create calamity; I am the LORD, who does all these things” (v. 7). The KJV rendered the last pair “I make peace, and create evil.” The text is addressed to *Cyrus*, a Persian king, whom God names his “anointed” (*mashiach*, “messiah”) — before Cyrus has even heard of Israel’s God. The speech is a rejection of every other power: no rival god, no devil, no cosmic dualism. Isaiah makes God the source of both sides. Then he doesn’t apologise.`],
d: [`**The context.** The prophet we call Second Isaiah writes in the 540s BCE, as Babylon falls to Persia. Persia’s state religion (Zoroastrianism, or its antecedents) had a dualism of a good god (Ahura Mazda) and an evil spirit (Angra Mainyu). Isaiah 45:7 reads as a deliberate answer: the One God is *both*. The verse about light and darkness inverts the Zoroastrian binary.`, `**Why that matters.** The strong monotheism of Isaiah 40–55 (“there is no other,” 45:5, 45:21) is probably the first unambiguous denial of other gods in the Bible. Earlier texts assume that other gods exist but are lesser. This monotheism is *born in exile*, after the destruction of the temple — a theology that insists that Israel’s catastrophe was God’s own work, not a defeat by stronger gods. It is a theodicy of last resort: if the only God is sovereign, Babylon is his tool.`, `**The cost.** If one God is the source of everything, you can’t hand evil off to a rival. The question of evil becomes personal: *Why did you do this?* Job, Lamentations 3, Ecclesiastes and Isaiah 45 form a spine in the Bible of a God who does not apologise for pain.`],
v: [[`Mark S. Smith`, `In *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism* (2001) he traces monotheism as a development, with its sharpest assertions in the exilic Isaiah, where it functions as a political theology: the nations’ gods are nothing; Yahweh, not Marduk, controls history.`], [`Zoroastrianism (Yasna 30)`, `The Gathas present two primal spirits, one choosing life and truth, the other death and the lie; humanity must choose a side. The moral clarity is its attraction; the cosmic dualism is its cost.`], [`Marcion and the Gnostics`, `Resolved the same problem the other way: the God of Isaiah 45:7 (creator of evil) cannot be the Father of Jesus; there must be two gods.`], [`Epicurus (as reported by Lactantius)`, `Is God willing to prevent evil but unable? Then he is not omnipotent. Able but not willing? Then he is malevolent. Both willing and able? Then whence evil? Isaiah answers: from God.`]],
b: [`This verse is the cornerstone of Mantz’s argument against the Devil: “it is the Absolute that ‘form[s] light and create[s] darkness, [who] bring[s] prosperity and create[s] calamity,’ not another, albeit negative spiritual entity.” For him, one God means one source; so *Mensch* alone bears the blame for separating from the Absolute.`, `Note what the verse doesn’t say: it doesn’t say *humans* are the source of evil, or that evil is merely perspective. It says the Absolute creates calamity. The book resolves the problem in two ways at once: evil as *privatio boni* (not a created thing) and evil as God’s creation (Isaiah 45:7). Those aren’t easily reconciled. And the Absolute’s “perfect goodness” requires something more than the claim that morality is “merely subjective.” What do you do with a God who creates calamity, if “goodness” is only a human perspective?`],
q: [`Is a God who is the source of both light and darkness more or less trustworthy than a God who is only light?`, `Which cost seems higher: believing in a cosmic evil being, or believing God is responsible for evil?`, `Cyrus, a pagan king, is called God’s messiah. What does that do to the idea that only Israel’s children are chosen?`],
s: [`God is the direct creator of calamity.`, `God allows but doesn’t create`, `God creates it, as Isaiah says`],
p: `Name one calamity from your own life. Say aloud, “God did this.” Then say, “God did not do this.” Notice which feels more like honesty.`,
src: [`Mark S. Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism* (Oxford, 2001)`, `Walter Brueggemann, *Isaiah 40–66* (Westminster Bible Companion, 1998)`, `Mary Boyce, *Zoroastrians* (1979)`, `Jon D. Levenson, *Creation and the Persistence of Evil* (1988)`]
},
{
t: `Whose Suffering?`, k: `Isaiah 53 · Reading the Servant`, ref: [`Isaiah 53:1-9`],
tag: `The most Christian chapter in the Old Testament — and a text Jews read very differently.`,
c: [`Isaiah 53 is a clear prophecy of Jesus’ death, written 700 years earlier.`],
f: [`The chapter is quoted or echoed in the New Testament (Acts 8:32–35; 1 Peter 2:22–25; Mark 10:45). But before it was a Christian text, it was a Jewish one; and most Jewish readers, from Rashi onward, read the Servant as *Israel*. They can point to Isaiah 49:3, where the LORD says, “You are my servant, Israel.” In its own context, the Servant is a figure in the exile, and the chapter doesn’t say “the Messiah” anywhere. It says “he” without a name. When Christians insist that 53 is plainly about Jesus, they are making a claim that Jewish tradition has resisted for two thousand years — and the Jewish claim isn’t anti-Christian; it’s a different reading of an ambiguous text.`],
d: [`**The Servant Songs.** Isaiah 42, 49, 50, 52:13–53:12 are four poems about a servant whose identity shifts: Israel (49:3), an individual with a mission to Israel (49:5–6), a teacher beaten for obedience (50:6), and finally the suffering one of 53. Whether these are four pictures of one figure is itself disputed.`, `**Substitution.** Isaiah 53 uses cultic language — “guilt offering” (*asham*, v. 10) — and a statement that the Servant bore others’ sin. It is the strongest Old Testament text for substitutionary atonement. Yet scholars often note that nowhere else does the Hebrew Bible say that one person’s death pays for another’s sin, which makes the chapter remarkable. Jesus’ early followers, scrambling to explain a crucified Messiah, found in Isaiah 53 the only text that made sense of it. Was that discovery or invention?`, `**The history of interpretation.** Some medieval rabbis (Rashi, Ibn Ezra) said Israel; some (the Targum Jonathan) said the Messiah; some Jewish mystics said the Messiah suffering. The Christian reading is not alien to Jewish history; neither is it the only possibility.`],
v: [[`Rashi (11th c.)`, `Reads the Servant as Israel, afflicted among the nations, “bearing” the sins of the nations whose punishment they ought to have faced — and who shall one day be vindicated.`], [`Targum Jonathan`, `The Aramaic paraphrase of the Prophets gives 52:13 as “Behold, my servant the Messiah shall prosper” — an early Jewish messianic reading, though it carefully avoids the suffering.`], [`Amy-Jill Levine`, `A Jewish New Testament scholar who urges Christians to stop saying that Judaism had no concept of suffering Messiah, and Jews to stop saying that Christians invented the reading from nothing.`], [`Philip the evangelist (Acts 8)`, `Meets an Ethiopian official reading Isaiah 53 and asks: “Do you understand?” The official says, “How can I, unless someone guides me?” — and asks whether the prophet is speaking of himself or of someone else.`]],
b: [`The book’s dénouement is startling: “The Christ’s death, therefore, had no inherent salvific attributes, but was merely a display of the Absolute’s desire to enter into relation with *Mensch*.” That is effectively a rejection of penal substitution — the very reading Isaiah 53 is most often used to support.`, `But elsewhere Mantz writes that the Christ “made possible salvation” and cites “penal substitution, and propitiation He achieved” as insufficient without the Spirit. The book seems to hold two views at once: the cross as objective achievement and as demonstration. Is that a consistent theory of the atonement? And if Isaiah 53 doesn’t mean what the New Testament says it means, what is the basis for trusting the New Testament’s reading of the Old at all?`],
q: [`If the original meaning of Isaiah 53 is about Israel, is the Christian reading a fulfilment or a reuse? Does it matter?`, `Does the doctrine of substitutionary atonement depend on this chapter? What other texts hold it?`, `What can a Christian learn from a Jewish reading of this chapter, and vice versa?`],
s: [`Isaiah 53 is directly about Jesus.`, `Not about Jesus at all`, `Directly about Jesus`],
p: `Read Isaiah 53 twice: once imagining it was written about a persecuted people, once about one man. Write down what changes.`,
src: [`John Goldingay, *Isaiah 40–55* (ICC, 2006)`, `Amy-Jill Levine & Marc Zvi Brettler, eds., *The Jewish Annotated New Testament* (Oxford, 2011)`, `Adolf Neubauer & S. R. Driver, *The Fifty-third Chapter of Isaiah According to the Jewish Interpreters* (1877)`, `N. T. Wright, *Jesus and the Victory of God* (1996)`]
},
{
t: `The Gods in the Room`, k: `Psalm 82 · John 10:34 · The divine council`, ref: [`Psalm 82`, `John 10:31-36`],
tag: `A psalm in which God stands in a heavenly court and sentences other gods to die.`,
c: [`The “gods” in Psalm 82 are human judges or rulers, and Jesus uses the verse to make a point about his own divinity.`],
f: [`Read verse 1 slowly: “God has taken his place in the divine council; in the midst of the *gods* he holds judgment.” The Hebrew is *elohim* — the same word for God. He then charges the gods with injustice and sentences them: “I said, ‘You are gods, sons of the Most High, all of you; nevertheless, like men you shall die.’” This is a courtroom in heaven, with a plurality of divine beings, one of whom (the speaker) is above the rest. It reads like a relic from an older worldview in which Yahweh was the chief of the gods. Jesus quotes the same verse in John 10:34, arguing that if scripture could call these beings “gods,” he can hardly be blamed for claiming to be God’s Son.`],
d: [`**The traditional explanation.** Many Christians read the “gods” of Psalm 82 as human judges (as Jewish tradition sometimes does) because it protects monotheism from the plain sense of the text.`, `**The scholarly explanation.** Most critical scholars (Mark S. Smith among them) and some evangelical ones (Michael Heiser) read it as a real divine council. Ugaritic texts depict El presiding over the assembly of the gods, with Baal and others as subordinate. In Israelite religion, Yahweh took the place of El (as in Deut 32:8–9), then displaced the other sons of El altogether. Psalm 82 is a snapshot of that process, in which God demotes the other deities to mortality — the theological equivalent of a trial in which a pantheon is dissolved.`, `**Deut 32:8–9.** The Dead Sea Scrolls reading says God “fixed the borders of the peoples according to the number of the *sons of God*” and Yahweh’s own portion was Jacob. That is the same picture: nations governed by lesser divine beings; Israel belonging to Yahweh directly. Paul’s “principalities and powers,” and the idea of national angels in Daniel 10, belong to this world.`],
v: [[`Ugaritic Baal Cycle (14th–12th c. BCE)`, `Describes El, the old high god, presiding over a council of divine sons, with Asherah as his consort. “Yahweh and his Asherah” also appears on inscriptions at Kuntillet Ajrud (c. 800 BCE), showing a religious world broader than later orthodoxy admits.`], [`William Dever, *Did God Have a Wife?* (2005)`, `Argues, from archaeology, that popular religion in ancient Israel included worship of Asherah, and that monotheism was largely an elite, late development.`], [`Athanasius (c. 360)`, `His famous line — God became human so that humans might become divine — has often been backed in Eastern theology by Psalm 82:6. Divinisation (theosis) became central to Orthodoxy.`], [`Jesus (John 10:34)`, `Quotes the psalm without embarrassment, in a debate about his own identity. He doesn’t treat it as a problem. Why would the claim to be God’s Son be easier to defend by appeal to a text about divine beings sentenced to death?`]],
b: [`Mantz’s “Note to the Critic” invokes the same verse: “When the Christ declared that ‘we are gods,’ believers almost instinctively remove themselves from a literal interpretation.” His point is that readers apply literalism inconsistently. The book’s argument is strictly monotheistic: “there can only exist one Necessary Existent.”`, `The pushback is that his own proof texts for strict monotheism (Isa 45) come late in a development; Psalm 82 is the older layer. If he insists on reading Psalm 82 non-literally because of a monotheism proven independently by reason, he is doing the same thing he accuses believers of: sorting texts by what they already conclude. The philosophical argument (one necessary being) and the historical one (what Israel actually believed) are different questions.`],
q: [`Does a divine council in Scripture threaten monotheism, or does it describe what “monotheism” originally meant?`, `If the nations’ gods are real but doomed, how should a Christian talk about other religions’ gods?`, `If Israel’s belief in God changed over time, does that undermine revelation or deepen it?`],
s: [`The Bible always taught strict monotheism.`, `It evolved over time`, `It was always one God alone`],
p: `Read Psalm 82 aloud, then Isaiah 45:5. Notice the distance between them. Write one sentence on what you would say to a friend who says “the Bible is consistent.”`,
src: [`Mark S. Smith, *The Early History of God* (Eerdmans, 2002)`, `Michael S. Heiser, *The Unseen Realm* (Lexham, 2015)`, `William G. Dever, *Did God Have a Wife?* (Eerdmans, 2005)`, `Simon B. Parker, ed., *Ugaritic Narrative Poetry* (Scholars Press, 1997)`]
},
{
t: `Sons of God`, k: `Deuteronomy 32 · Genesis 6 · Enoch`, ref: [`Deuteronomy 32:6-9`, `Genesis 6:1-4`],
tag: `Angels mating with women. A Bible verse quoted by the New Testament from a book that’s not in the Bible.`,
c: [`The “sons of God” in Genesis 6 are the godly line of Seth, intermarrying with the line of Cain.`],
f: [`That reading, popularised by Augustine, is the standard conservative one — but it’s a late harmonisation. The early Jewish reading — 1 Enoch, Jubilees, the Dead Sea Scrolls, Josephus, Philo — is overwhelmingly that the “sons of God” are angels, and the “Nephilim” their giant offspring. Jude 6 and 2 Peter 2:4 clearly assume this tradition; Jude 14–15 *quotes* 1 Enoch 1:9 as prophecy. The Bible’s own authors treated a book most Christians have never read as authoritative.`],
d: [`**The Book of the Watchers.** 1 Enoch 1–36, composed in the third century BCE, expands Genesis 6: 200 angels descend on Mount Hermon, take human wives, teach metalworking, enchantments, cosmetics, and astrology. Their giant offspring devour humanity; the Flood is God’s response. The surviving spirits of the giants become demons. This story is arguably the origin of the New Testament’s demonology (the “unclean spirits” who inhabit people), and it’s why the Flood story in Jewish tradition is linked with the origin of evil.`, `**Why it was cut.** The Ethiopian Orthodox Church still treats 1 Enoch as Scripture. Western churches dropped it perhaps because Augustine and others found it embarrassing; the rabbis excluded it for reasons that likely included its angelology and its use by Christians. Among the Dead Sea Scrolls, about a dozen manuscripts of Enoch appear — more than many canonical books.`, `**Implications.** If Jude quotes Enoch, is Enoch Scripture? Most Protestants say Jude quoted a source without endorsing the whole (as Paul quoted pagan poets in Acts 17:28). But Jude introduces the quote with “Enoch … prophesied.” The line between canon and non-canon, in the first century, was far less sharp than later.`],
v: [[`1 Enoch 6–11`, `Says that Shemihazah led two hundred angels who swore an oath on Mount Hermon to take human wives; Azazel taught weapons and cosmetics; the earth cried out; God sent the archangels to bind them under the earth until the judgment.`], [`Augustine, *City of God* 15`, `Rejects the angel reading because angels are spirits and cannot procreate; he interprets “sons of God” as Seth’s godly descendants.`], [`Origen, *Against Celsus*`, `Reports that the book of Enoch is not accepted as divine in the churches, which tells us some did accept it.`], [`Michael Heiser`, `Argues the angel reading is the original, and that the New Testament’s demonology is Deut 32/Enoch’s worldview made explicit.`]],
b: [`The book insists that an extracanonical text “ought to be read in conjunction with eclectic works such as the bible to retain a better understanding.” Mantz argues the Bible “is a library of different books … at least forty various authors,” and that extracanonical texts help recover context.`, `He also denies the angelic and demonic host exist at all: “Al-Shayṭān, along with the demonic and angelic hosts, does not … exist.” But if Enoch is the context that makes sense of Jude, 2 Peter and the demonology of the Gospels, then rejecting its worldview means rejecting the context he says we need. Which extracanonical texts do we keep as interpretive aids — and which parts of their worldview do we discard — and by what principle?`],
q: [`If Jude treated Enoch as prophecy, on what basis do we decide it isn’t?`, `Which is more disturbing to you — angels with human children, or a Bible that edits them out?`, `If you found out that an ancient book you’d never heard of shaped the New Testament’s worldview, would it change how you read the New Testament?`],
s: [`Enoch should be in the Bible.`, `No: rightly excluded`, `Yes: should be canonical`],
p: `Read 1 Enoch 1:9 online next to Jude 14–15. Write down in your own words what the comparison shows.`,
src: [`George W. E. Nickelsburg & James VanderKam, *1 Enoch: A New Translation* (Fortress, 2004)`, `Lee Martin McDonald, *The Biblical Canon* (Hendrickson, 2007)`, `Michael S. Heiser, *Reversing Hermon* (2017)`, `R. H. Charles, *The Book of Enoch* (1912/1917; public domain)`]
}
];
